# stair-route-probe — CAN A PERSON WALK FROM THE FOOT OF THE GRAND STAIR TO EVERY ROOM ON THE
#                     PIANO NOBILE? Measured, as shapes, against the assembled building.
#
# stair.clip's own `stair_probe_head` tests THIS PART AGAINST ITSELF and says so in its own words:
# "Something another fragment hangs over the passage later will not show up here." A ROUTE is
# exactly that case — it leaves the stair at the gallery's south edge and crosses two other
# fragments before it reaches a room — so it cannot be measured from inside any one file. This is
# the manifest that measures it.
#
# WHAT IS ON THE PIANO NOBILE, and it is a short list. There are four rooms at y = 6.30 in this
# building and no others: the two stair galleries (stair.clip, north strip of each wing), the two
# picture galleries (enfilade.clip, east and west strips), the BALLROOM (east, south front) and the
# LIBRARY (west, south front). vestibule.clip stops at 6.30, rotunda.clip has no gallery, halls.clip
# is solid to 6.975, and terrace.clip is on the roof at 11.75 with no stair to it. So the whole
# question is: from the top of the grand stair, can you reach the picture gallery, the ballroom and
# the library — and the same three in the west wing.
#
# TWO KINDS OF MEASUREMENT, AND THE SECOND IS THE ONE NOTHING ELSE MAKES
#
#   `sr_head_*`   the volume a person sweeps through — 2.40 m tall, and as wide as the building
#                 actually gives — INTERSECTED with the assembled building. MUST BE EMPTY.
#                 Anything it reports is something standing in the way.
#
#   `sr_floor_*`  a skin 0.10 m thick immediately under the walking surface, MINUS the assembled
#                 building. MUST BE EMPTY. Anything it reports is a place with NO FLOOR — which is
#                 the fault this whole probe exists for, and which a head-height check cannot see
#                 at all: a doorway opening onto a four-metre drop measures as perfectly clear.
#
# HOW TO RUN IT
#
#   bash tools/clipcheck.sh clips/facility/requests/stair-route-probe.clip --metre 16 --part <name>
#   bash tools/clipcheck.sh clips/facility/requests/stair-route-probe.clip --metre 32 --part <name>
#
# WHAT EACH ONE SHOULD SAY, as it stands on 2026-08-17, at metre 16. Two of them are NOT empty and
# both readings are somebody else's member, named, measured and written up in requests/stair.md §8:
#
#   sr_head_all     56 voxels     library.clip's lower cornice frieze, soffit 8.315, crossing its
#                                 own doorway at 2.015 m clear (§8.5). Everything else EMPTY.
#                                 At METRE 32 the same part is 486 voxels in ONE component, all
#                                 `gilt`, at x -14.844..-14.000, y 8.312..8.406, z -3.594..-3.406 —
#                                 the same member, eight times as many voxels, nothing else.
#   sr_head_foot    EMPTY         the passage at the foot of the stair, 1.22 x 2.40
#   sr_head_rake    EMPTY         the twenty-three treads, the half landing and the gallery
#   sr_head_brg     EMPTY         the gallery's east end and the bridge, 1.29 x 2.40
#   sr_head_gall    EMPTY         the picture gallery, 1.00 x 2.40, both wings
#   sr_head_door    56            the two doorways, at the aperture each of them actually has
#   sr_floor_all    174 voxels    the 23 mm the room slabs and enfilade.clip's do not lap (§8.3).
#                                 It was 1,922 before the bridge.
#   sr_floor_join   the same 174, on its own, so it cannot be mistaken for anything else
#   sr_thresh_e     MATTER, in TWO components — the control arm for the line above
#   sr_cut_check    EMPTY         nothing of part_stair inside anybody's void
#   sr_who_lib      56            whose stone the 56 are; the other four sr_who_* are EMPTY
#
# TWO OF THESE HAVE NOT BEEN TAKEN AT METRE 32 AND THAT IS SAID RATHER THAN ASSUMED: `sr_floor_all`
# and `sr_cut_check` are metre 16 readings only. Both are expected to hold — the 23 mm the floor
# slabs miss each other by is smaller than a voxel at either metre, and `sr_cut_check` is exact
# geometry rather than a sampling question, because this part's top face IS the plane the void
# starts at. Run them when there is a machine free; the box is 79 million cells at 32 and each run
# is the better part of an hour.
#
# THE ASSEMBLY BELOW IS THE MANIFEST'S, NOT A SIMPLIFICATION. clips/facility.clip subtracts the
# room voids and the opening voids from `built` and then unions the fittings back after the ROOM
# voids but still cut by the OPENING voids. A route probe that skipped that would test a building
# nobody ships: `void_windows` alone deletes matter 1.00 m in from every outer face, and the last
# 0.10 m of both picture galleries is inside it.
#
# `metre` and `bounds` come AFTER EVERY include and not before — every fragment includes
# _contract.clip again and the contract re-states the building's own 34 x 21 x 25 m box, which at
# metre 32 is 582 million cells. The box below is the two north-south strips, both wings, plus the
# stair, which is 15% of it.

include "../_contract.clip"
include "../_order.clip"

paint limestone

include "../podium.clip"
include "../walls.clip"
include "../windows.clip"
include "../doors.clip"
include "../halls.clip"
include "../stair.clip"
include "../enfilade.clip"
include "../ballroom.clip"
include "../library.clip"
include "../fittings.clip"

let sr_built = union {
    part_podium part_walls part_windows part_doors
    part_halls part_stair part_enfilade part_ballroom part_library
}
let sr_rooms = difference {
    sr_built
    void_halls void_stair void_ballroom void_library void_enfilade
}
let sr_all = union {
    difference { sr_rooms void_doors void_windows }
    difference { part_fittings void_doors void_windows }
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE ROUTE, EAST WING — foot of the stair, up, along the gallery, over the bridge, through the
#                        picture gallery, into the ballroom
# ================================================================================================
#
# Every box below is 2.40 m tall, standing on the surface a foot is on, and as wide as the building
# gives at that point. Where a box is narrower than the 1.20 m a circulation route wants, the
# number is written beside it and the reason is in requests/stair.md.

# 1. THE FOOT. The passage under the stair, east of the first riser at x = 13.54 and its newel
#    (which reaches 13.6525), north of enfilade.clip's ground block (which reaches z 3.115), west of
#    walls.clip's inner face at 15.10. 1.22 m wide.
let sr_foot_e = box 13.66 1.80 3.16   14.88 4.20 6.44

# 2. THE FLIGHTS AND THE LANDING are stair.clip's own per-tread volumes, re-asked here against the
#    WHOLE building rather than against the stair alone — which is a sterner question and the one
#    that would catch a fragment hanging something over the well.
let sr_rake_e = union { stair_probe_ra stair_probe_rb stair_probe_land stair_probe_gall }

# 3. THE BRIDGE AND THE TURN. The east end of the gallery, the crossing of its south balustrade
#    line, and the bridge south to enfilade.clip's north archway. 1.31 m wide in x, between the
#    bridge's own balustrade (which reaches 13.7025) and walls.clip's inner face at 15.10. Its south
#    end stops at z = 3.13, which is 0.015 north of enfilade.clip's west wall (3.115).
#
#    IT IS THE SAME NUMBERS AS `stair_probe_brg` IN stair.clip AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. There the box
#    is intersected with the stair alone and says nothing of MINE is in the way; here it is
#    intersected with the assembled building and says nothing of ANYBODY'S is. One shape, two
#    questions, and the second is the only one that means anything on a bridge whose two ends are
#    other people's stone.
#    THE EAST LIMIT IS 15.04 AND NOT 15.06, AND THAT IS A MEASUREMENT. At 15.06 this box came back
#    with 168 voxels a side at metre 16 and 2 a side at metre 8: enfilade.clip's own architrave
#    round the east light at z = 2.70 stands 0.045 proud of walls.clip's face at 15.10, so its jamb
#    at z 3.3745..3.4805 and its head at y 8.542..8.648 reach x = 15.047 — and BOTH of them are
#    NORTH of enfilade.clip's own end face at 3.115, standing in the stair well rather than in the
#    passage they belong to. They take nothing off this route: the clear here is 13.7025 to 15.047,
#    which is 1.3445 m. The box is 1.29 and clears the architrave by 7 mm.
let sr_brg_e = box 13.75 6.30 3.13    15.04 8.70 6.52
let sr_brg_w = box -15.04 6.30 3.13  -13.75 8.70 6.52

# 4. THROUGH THE PICTURE GALLERY, AND THIS LEG IS 1.00 m AND NOT 1.20, WHICH IS THE ONE PLACE ON
#    THE WHOLE ROUTE WHERE THE BUILDING IS UNDER THE CIRCULATION FIGURE. The strip is 1.15 m of
#    stone to stone — halls.clip's block face at 13.95 against walls.clip's inner face at 15.10 —
#    and enfilade.clip's own words for it are "every millimetre there is". 1.20 does not fit between
#    those two faces at all, so this is arithmetic and not a measurement. What IS measured is what
#    stands inside the 1.15: the base course cap to 13.995 and 15.055, the impost to the same two at
#    y 8.685, and the girandoles and sconces to 14.045. 14.05 .. 15.05 is what is left, and it is
#    enfilade.clip's own `enf_walk_u` band asked 0.30 m taller — 2.40 rather than 2.10.
#
#    A FIRST CUT OF THIS BOX WAS 14.00 .. 15.05 AND IT WAS NOT EMPTY: `sr_head_all` carried 364
#    voxels of `wax`, `taper`, `gilt` and `ormolu` at metre 16, which are the girandole arms and
#    their flames standing at 14.045. That is the measurement behind the 0.045, and it is why the
#    request in stair.md asks enfilade.clip for the WALL rather than for the fittings: the fittings
#    are already pulled back as far as that file can pull them.
let sr_gall_e = box 14.05 6.30 -2.90   15.05 8.70 3.16

# 5. THROUGH THE BALLROOM DOOR — 0.89 m WIDE BECAUSE THAT IS ALL THERE IS, AND 2.10 TALL BECAUSE
#    THAT IS THE FIGURE AN OPENING IS HELD TO. Every other box here is 2.40, which is the figure for
#    circulation; a doorway is allowed 2.10 and asking it for 2.40 would report the two state rooms'
#    own cornices as faults when they are not.
#
#    ballroom.clip cuts 13.725 .. 14.85 and calls it 1.125 (2.5 M) — but the west 0.225 of that opens
#    onto enfilade.clip's own wall face at 13.95, which stands across it from z = -2.965 northward,
#    so the APERTURE a person walks through is 13.95 .. 14.85 = 0.90 m. That is 0.10 under the 1.00 m
#    an opening is held to. The request is in requests/stair.md. The box is the aperture less 5 mm
#    a side.
let sr_door_e = box 13.955 6.30 -3.60   14.845 8.40 -2.88

# ================================================================================================
# THE ROUTE, WEST WING — the same, and NOT a mirror of it: the library is not the ballroom
# ================================================================================================
#
# stair.clip and enfilade.clip are both `mirror { } axis=x`, so the west halves of 1 to 4 are the
# same shapes folded. The library's door is not, and it fails the 1.00 m in a DIFFERENT WAY from the
# ballroom's, which is what makes the pair of them worth stating together.
#
# `library_door` is x -15.00 .. -13.95, 1.05 m, and every millimetre of it is clear on the CORRIDOR
# side — its east jamb is flush with the halls block, so unlike the ballroom's nothing of
# enfilade.clip stands in it. It is the ROOM side that closes it:
#
#   -14.85   `library_room`, the library's own air, begins here — the west 0.15 m of the doorway
#            opens onto that room's own 0.25 m of west lining, floor to vault.
#   -13.995  `lib_low_ends`, the board that closes the press carcase where the doorway cuts it,
#            over z -4.08 .. -3.40 and floor to 8.325: the last 45 mm of the east side.
#
# So the aperture into the library is -14.85 .. -13.995 = 0.855 m, and the box below is 0.847.
# A 1.00 m box caught 488 voxels at metre 16 — ALL of it `part_library`'s own stone, which is what
# `sr_who_lib` says and what `sr_who_enf`, `sr_who_wal`, `sr_who_hal` and `sr_who_fit` deny.
#
# THE TWO STATE ROOMS FAIL THE SAME FIGURE FOR OPPOSITE REASONS: the ballroom's door is blind on
# the corridor side against somebody else's wall, the library's is blind on the room side against
# its own. Both give about 0.87 m where 1.00 is asked for.
#
# AND THE LIBRARY'S BOX IS 2.10 TALL AND STILL NOT EMPTY — 56 voxels of `gilt` at metre 16, which is
# `lib_lco_frieze`, the library's own lower cornice frieze, running unbroken across its own doorway.
# Its outer box is `-14.90 8.325 -6.65 -6.70 8.415 -3.40 round=0.010`, so its soffit is 8.315 and the
# clear height at that threshold is 2.015 m. THAT READING IS LEFT IN rather than tuned out: it is a
# real member at a real height on the only route into that room, and it is exactly the fault
# enfilade.clip warns about at the head of its own trim section — "salon.clip measured 822 voxels of
# its own skirting, dado cap and cornice standing straight across its own doorway, which nothing in a
# component count, a volume or a material list will ever report". See requests/stair.md §8.5.
let sr_foot_w = box -14.88 1.80 3.16   -13.66 4.20 6.44
let sr_gall_w = box -15.05 6.30 -2.90   -14.05 8.70 3.16
let sr_door_w = box -14.845 6.30 -3.60   -13.998 8.40 -2.88

# ================================================================================================
# THE FIVE HEAD-HEIGHT PARTS — four of these report an EMPTY CLIP and the fifth is §8.5
#
# The five boxes are disjoint, so `sr_head_all` is exactly their sum and a reading in it can be put
# in one leg by its worldbox alone. Run the legs when the sum is not zero.
# ================================================================================================

let sr_head_foot  = intersection { sr_all union { sr_foot_e sr_foot_w } }
let sr_head_rake  = intersection { sr_all sr_rake_e }
let sr_head_brg   = intersection { sr_all union { sr_brg_e sr_brg_w } }
let sr_head_gall  = intersection { sr_all union { sr_gall_e sr_gall_w } }
let sr_head_door  = intersection { sr_all union { sr_door_e sr_door_w } }

let sr_head_all = intersection {
    sr_all
    union { sr_foot_e sr_foot_w sr_rake_e sr_brg_e sr_brg_w sr_gall_e sr_gall_w sr_door_e sr_door_w }
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE FLOOR PARTS — a skin 0.10 m under the walking surface, MINUS the building. EMPTY or there is
#                   no floor there.
# ================================================================================================
#
# 6.12 .. 6.22 is inside every floor slab on this storey: stair.clip's gallery is 5.895 .. 6.30,
# enfilade.clip's is 6.00 .. 6.30 and ballroom.clip's and library.clip's are 6.00 .. 6.30.
#
# THE SKINS STOP ON THE FRAGMENT FACES AND ONE DOES NOT, ON PURPOSE. `sr_floor_join` straddles the
# joint between ballroom.clip's floor slab (which ends at z -2.988 once its round=0.012 is taken)
# and enfilade.clip's (which begins at -2.965). Those two do not touch: there is 23 mm of nothing
# between them, closed only by the fact that a voxel is solid if any of it is. It is measured here
# by itself so it cannot be mistaken for the bridge.

let sr_floor_gall_e = difference { box 13.80 6.12 4.70   15.05 6.22 6.50   sr_all }
let sr_floor_brg_e  = difference { box 13.80 6.12 3.16   15.05 6.22 4.70   sr_all }
let sr_floor_pic_e  = difference { box 14.00 6.12 -2.90  15.05 6.22 3.16   sr_all }
let sr_floor_room_e = difference { box 13.97 6.12 -3.60  14.83 6.22 -2.94  sr_all }

let sr_floor_gall_w = difference { box -15.05 6.12 4.70  -13.80 6.22 6.50  sr_all }
let sr_floor_brg_w  = difference { box -15.05 6.12 3.16  -13.80 6.22 4.70  sr_all }
let sr_floor_pic_w  = difference { box -15.05 6.12 -2.90 -14.00 6.22 3.16  sr_all }
let sr_floor_room_w = difference { box -14.98 6.12 -3.60 -13.97 6.22 -2.94 sr_all }

let sr_floor_all = difference {
    union {
        box 13.80 6.12 3.16   15.05 6.22 6.50
        box 14.00 6.12 -2.90  15.05 6.22 3.16
        box 13.97 6.12 -3.60  14.83 6.22 -2.94
        box -15.05 6.12 3.16  -13.80 6.22 6.50
        box -15.05 6.12 -2.90 -14.00 6.22 3.16
        box -14.98 6.12 -3.60 -13.97 6.22 -2.94
    }
    sr_all
}

let sr_floor_join = difference {
    union {
        box 13.97 6.12 -3.10   14.83 6.22 -2.90
        box -14.98 6.12 -3.10 -13.97 6.22 -2.90
    }
    sr_all
}

# AND THE CONTROL ARM FOR IT, which is the same box the other way round: what IS there rather than
# what is not. A difference against a CSG tree this deep is a bound and not an exact field, so a
# reading of 174 voxels on a 23 mm gap is a number that has to be checked from the other side before
# it is believed. `sr_thresh_*` is EXPECTED TO REPORT MATTER — a slab 0.86 x 0.10 x 0.40 m of it —
# and its z extent is the answer: if it runs the full -3.30 .. -2.70 the floor is continuous in
# voxels and the 23 mm is an SDF gap that a voxel closes; if it stops at -2.988 the enfilade's slab
# is genuinely not there and the doorway has a step at its threshold.
let sr_thresh_e = intersection { sr_all box 13.97 6.12 -3.30   14.83 6.22 -2.70 }
let sr_thresh_w = intersection { sr_all box -14.98 6.12 -3.30 -13.97 6.22 -2.70 }

# ================================================================================================
# WHOSE STONE IS IT — the same box asked of one part at a time
# ================================================================================================
#
# `sr_head_door` came back with 488 voxels at metre 16 and every one of them in the WEST doorway. A
# volume of the assembled building says THAT something is in the way and never WHOSE, and on a route
# that crosses four fragments that is the only question worth asking next. So the same box is
# intersected with one part at a time; exactly one of these reports matter.
let sr_who_lib = intersection { part_library   sr_door_w }
let sr_who_enf = intersection { part_enfilade  sr_door_w }
let sr_who_wal = intersection { part_walls     sr_door_w }
let sr_who_hal = intersection { part_halls     sr_door_w }
let sr_who_fit = intersection { part_fittings  sr_door_w }

# ================================================================================================
# THE CUTTER CHECK — the fault that has no symptom
# ================================================================================================
#
# `void_enfilade` is the air of the picture gallery and the cabinet, and the manifest subtracts it
# from the whole building AFTER assembly. stair.clip's bridge lands 0.05 into enfilade.clip's floor
# slab at z = 3.05, which is 0.05 from the mouth of its north archway (`enf_dr_un`, y 6.30 .. 8.70,
# x 13.95 .. 15.10, z 3.00 .. 3.15). Anything of the stair's inside that box is deleted with no
# error, no floating voxel and no change in any count — the same fault the arched north window was
# taking out of the half landing for the whole life of stair.clip.
#
#     --part sr_cut_check      -> "the clip is empty"
let sr_cut_check = intersection { part_stair union { void_enfilade void_ballroom void_library } }

metre 32
bounds -15.40 1.70 -3.90   15.40 8.90 7.00

solid sr_all
